  "content": {
    "id": "p32-q33-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "33",
    "prompt": "In $\\triangle ABC$, the sides have lengths $4$, $x$, and $y$ (in centimetres), with a fixed perimeter $4+x+y=12$. Let $\\theta$ be the angle opposite the side of length $y$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p32-q33-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\cos\\theta=2-\\dfrac{6}{x}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\cos\\theta=2-\\dfrac{6}{x}$.\n\nSubstitute $y=8-x$ into the rearranged cosine rule and simplify.",
          "worked": "The perimeter gives $y=8-x$. By the cosine rule,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{16+x^2-(8-x)^2}{8x}\\\\\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{16x-48}{8x}\\\\\\cos\\theta&=2-\\frac{6}{x}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q33-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, show that $2<x<6$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2<x<6$.\n\nUse $-1<\\cos\\theta<1$ and $x>0$ to solve the double inequality.",
          "worked": "Since $\\theta$ is an interior triangle angle, $-1<\\cos\\theta<1$. Also, $x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}-1&<2-\\frac{6}{x}<1\\\\1&<\\frac{6}{x}<3\\\\x&<6<3x.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $x<6$ and $x>2$, so $2<x<6$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      }
    ]
  },
